PR #2769  overall: compliant
Spec: Removing impl-def-behavior from MSTATUS_UXL_LEGAL_VALS_SMODE hardens the legal UXL value set so S-mode cannot operate under an unintended XLEN that would misinterpret U-mode virtual addresses and breach the S/U privilege boundary.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/process.c:172-179  start_thread
      Clears SR_UXL then writes only SR_UXL_32 or SR_UXL_64 into the in-memory regs->status. These are the only two architecturally-defined legal UXL encodings (UXL=01 and UXL=10) per the RISC-V privileged spec. The value is later written to hardware sstatus in entry.S:301 (csrw CSR_STATUS, a0), where sstatus.UXL is read-only — the hardware ignores UXL writes to sstatus and instead shadows mstatus.UXL set by firmware. The in-memory regs->status UXL bits are consumed by syscall_handler (syscall.h:106) for compat routing. No risk of writing an impl-def value because (a) only the two spec-defined values are ever used, and (b) sstatus.UXL is hardware read-only anyway.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/process.c:126-140  compat_mode_detect
      Boot-time probe writes SR_UXL_32 to CSR_STATUS then reads back to detect compat support. On S-mode Linux, CSR_STATUS = CSR_SSTATUS, and sstatus.UXL is a read-only shadow of mstatus.UXL. The write to sstatus.UXL is silently ignored by hardware, so the probe is actually testing the mstatus.UXL value pre-configured by M-mode firmware, not whether the implementation supports UXL=32 in sstatus. This means compat mode availability depends on firmware configuration rather than hardware capability — if firmware sets mstatus.UXL=64, compat is silently disabled regardless of hardware support. Not a security violation against the spec (only arch-defined UXL values are ever read), but the correctness of compat mode availability depends on an unverified external invariant (firmware UXL configuration).
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/syscall.h:105-107  syscall_handler
      Reads (regs->status & SR_UXL) and compares against SR_UXL_32 to route compat vs native syscalls. This is a pure consumer — it reads the UXL bits captured from hardware sstatus at trap entry (which reflects mstatus.UXL set by firmware). If UXL were somehow an unknown/illegal value, the code defaults to the native (64-bit) syscall table via the else branch, which is the safe fallback. No write path, no TOCTOU window (UXL is invariant for a process lifetime).
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/csr.h:62-66  
      Defines SR_UXL = 0x300000000 (bits 32-33), SR_UXL_32 = 0x100000000 (UXL=01), SR_UXL_64 = 0x200000000 (UXL=10). These encodings exactly match the RISC-V privileged architecture spec: UXL is bits [33:32] of mstatus/sstatus, with 01=XLEN32, 10=XLEN64. Only the two architecturally-valid UXL values are defined; no constant is provided for UXL=00 (illegal on RV64) or UXL=11 (reserved). The bitmask and constants correctly restrict all write sites to only legal values.
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/csr.h:415-415  
      Defines CSR_MSTATUS = 0x300, the canonical mstatus CSR number per the RISC-V privileged spec. This is a reference constant; all real UXL manipulation goes through CSR_STATUS (aliased to CSR_SSTATUS under S-mode or CSR_MSTATUS under M-mode). The constant value is architecturally correct.
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/csr.h:487-488  
      Under CONFIG_RISCV_M_MODE, aliases CSR_STATUS to CSR_MSTATUS. For typical S-mode Linux kernels (CONFIG_RISCV_M_MODE=n), CSR_STATUS aliases to CSR_SSTATUS (csr.h:513). The M-mode alias is irrelevant for standard Linux — it only applies to M-mode firmware builds. In both cases the CSR alias is architecturally correct, and all code sites uniformly use CSR_STATUS, so the alias is transparent.

