PR #2769  overall: bug_confirmed
Spec: Removing impl-def-behavior from MSTATUS_UXL_LEGAL_VALS_SMODE hardens the legal UXL value set so S-mode cannot operate under an unintended XLEN that would misinterpret U-mode virtual addresses and breach the S/U privilege boundary.
  [RISK] include/sbi/riscv_encoding.h:38-41  (global macros)
      MSTATUS_UXL, MSTATUS_SXL, and MSTATUS_SBE are defined only as raw bitmasks under #if __riscv_xlen == 64. No UXL_SHIFT, no LEGAL_VALS mask, and no UXL encodings for RV64 (1=XLEN32, 2=XLEN64) are defined anywhere in this header. This is the root definition site. Every downstream function that reads or writes mstatus/sstatus uses these macros only to isolate the field position, never to validate field values. The absence of architectural-legal-value constants precludes write-side validation.
  [RISK] include/sbi/riscv_encoding.h:71-71  (global macros)
      SSTATUS64_UXL is a direct alias of MSTATUS_UXL. This is the S-mode-visible view of the UXL field through sstatus. OpenSBI swaps sstatus wholesale via csr_swap() in domain context switches and never masks or validates the UXL field within sstatus. The alias inherits the absence of legal-value constants from MSTATUS_UXL, leaving the sstatus read/write path equally unguarded.
  [BUG] lib/sbi/sbi_hart.c:32-48  mstatus_init
      mstatus_init writes mstatus_val=0 to CSR_MSTATUS via csr_write(CSR_MSTATUS, mstatus_val) at line 48. This encodes UXL=0 in bits 32:33. On RV64, the ISA manual requires UXL to be 1 (XLEN=32) or 2 (XLEN=64); value 0 is architecturally illegal. While mstatus.UXL is WARL and hardware MAY reject write of 0, the rejection behavior itself was 'implementation-defined' under the prior spec wording. OpenSBI never explicitly sets UXL to a known-legal encoding at boot—it implicitly relies on hardware WARL to preserve the reset value. Called from sbi_hart_reinit() on both cold boot and warm resume.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_hart.c:768-807  sbi_hart_switch_mode
      sbi_hart_switch_mode is the sole C-level privilege-mode switch path. It reads CSR_MSTATUS (line 794), uses INSERT_FIELD to modify only MPP (line 795) and MPIE (line 796), and conditionally MPV (line 805), then writes back via csr_write(CSR_MSTATUS, val) at line 807. UXL and SXL are read-modify-write preserved without any legal-value check. The correctness of the target mode's XLEN depends entirely on the assumption that every prior writer (mstatus_init, TRAP_RESTORE_MEPC_MSTATUS, domain context restore) wrote a valid UXL/SXL encoding. If a prior debugger, bootloader stage, or domain context stored an implementation-defined illegal UXL, it propagates into the switched-to mode.
  [RISK] firmware/fw_base.S:647-660  TRAP_RESTORE_MEPC_MSTATUS
      TRAP_RESTORE_MEPC_MSTATUS is the assembly macro that restores mstatus from the on-stack trap context before mret. It loads the saved raw mstatus value via REG_L t0, SBI_TRAP_REGS_OFFSET(mstatus)(a0) and writes it directly to hardware via csrw CSR_MSTATUS, t0 (line 657). This is the terminal hardware-write point for mstatus on every trap return. No bitwise mask or field validation is applied to UXL (bits 32:33). Any illegal UXL value captured by TRAP_SAVE_MEPC_MSTATUS, preserved through sbi_trap_redirect, or stored by domain_context memcpy is written verbatim to the physical CSR. A single-point fix here would cover all trap-return paths.
  [OK] firmware/fw_base.S:531-543  TRAP_SAVE_MEPC_MSTATUS
      TRAP_SAVE_MEPC_MSTATUS captures CSR_MSTATUS (and conditionally CSR_MSTATUSH) into the on-stack sbi_trap_regs structure at trap entry. This is a pure capture operation—it reads whatever UXL value is present in hardware and stores it. The captured value is later restored by TRAP_RESTORE_MEPC_MSTATUS, but the save macro itself introduces no vulnerability. The risk lies in the absence of validation between save and restore.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_trap.c:103-231  sbi_trap_redirect
      sbi_trap_redirect modifies regs->mstatus fields using bitmask operations that all operate on bits outside UXL: MSTATUS_MPP (bits 11:12), MSTATUS_SPP (bit 8), MSTATUS_SPIE (bit 5), MSTATUS_SIE (bit 1), MSTATUS_MPV (bits 39 on RV64). UXL at bits 32:33 is never masked, cleared, or validated. The regs->mstatus value—including any illegal UXL carried in from the trapped context—is later restored verbatim into CSR_MSTATUS by TRAP_RESTORE_MEPC_MSTATUS at mret. Both the VS-mode redirect path (lines 165-199) and S-mode redirect path (lines 200-229) exhibit identical behavior.Wrote 3 report(s) → output/riscv-isa-manual/pr-2769/

  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_domain_context.c:102-169  switch_to_next_domain_context
      switch_to_next_domain_context saves and restores sstatus wholesale via csr_swap(CSR_SSTATUS, dom_ctx->sstatus) at line 131. The sstatus register on RV64 contains UXL in bits 32:33 (aliased as SSTATUS64_UXL). If dom_ctx->sstatus was saved from a domain that ran with an implementation-defined illegal UXL value, or if it was newly allocated with sstatus=0 (UXL=0), that value is restored directly into hardware CSR without validation. Additionally, the full trap context (including mstatus) is copied via memcpy at lines 149-151, preserving any illegal UXL in the trap-register state. For uninitialized target domains, sbi_hart_switch_mode() is called at line 160, which also performs no UXL validation.
  [BUG] lib/sbi/sbi_domain_context.c:193-227  sbi_domain_context_enter
      sbi_domain_context_enter is the entry point for domain context switching. On first invocation for a hart, it calls hart_context_init() at line 209, which allocates hart_context structs via sbi_zalloc() at line 181. This zero-initializes all fields including sstatus=0, which encodes UXL=0. On RV64, UXL=0 is architecturally illegal (must be 1 or 2). When switch_to_next_domain_context() later does csr_swap(CSR_SSTATUS, dom_ctx->sstatus) restoring UXL=0 into sstatus, the S-mode domain sees an illegal UXL value. On subsequent domain entries, the previously saved unvalidated sstatus (including whatever UXL was present) is restored without re-validation at line 226. This is the S-mode-visible attack surface for UXL corruption across domain boundaries.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_hsm.c:139-158  sbi_hsm_hart_start_finish
      sbi_hsm_hart_start_finish is the cold-boot, warm-startup, and hart-start entry point. It extracts next_addr, next_mode, next_arg1 from scratch, then delegates to sbi_hart_switch_mode() at line 157. This function itself performs no CSR manipulation—it is a pure caller. The UXL vulnerability is confined to the callee (sbi_hart_switch_mode) and the boot-time mstatus initialization (mstatus_init). This location is correctly flagged as a propagation point but is not itself the source of the defect.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_hsm.c:464-484  sbi_hsm_hart_resume_finish
      sbi_hsm_hart_resume_finish is called on warm resume after suspend. It calls __sbi_hsm_suspend_non_ret_restore() at line 479, which restores mideleg, medeleg, and menvcfg but does NOT restore or validate mstatus UXL/SXL fields. Then it calls sbi_hart_switch_mode() at lines 481-483, which performs an unvalidated read-modify-write of mstatus. After a suspend/resume cycle, the mstatus value present in hardware after warm-boot reinitialization may carry a UXL value that differs from the pre-suspend value—no cross-cycle consistency check exists.
  [OK] include/sbi/sbi_trap.h:129-204  (struct sbi_trap_regs)
      The sbi_trap_regs struct embeds 'unsigned long mstatus' at line 202 and 'unsigned long mstatusH' at line 204. There is no separate UXL field—UXL is only addressable as bits 32:33 within the raw mstatus word. This is architecturally correct (UXL is part of mstatus), but it means no code path can validate UXL independently of the full mstatus value without explicit bit extraction. The struct definition itself is not buggy; the vulnerability is in the code paths that save/restore it without field validation.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_unpriv.c:23-66  DEFINE_UNPRIVILEGED_LOAD_FUNCTION / DEFINE_UNPRIVILEGED_STORE_FUNCTION
      The unprivileged load/store inline assembly uses csrrs to read-and-set MPRV in mstatus (line 33/59), saving the original mstatus value into the 'mstatus' local variable. After the unprivileged access, csrw restores the saved mstatus at line 38/64. The value written back is the same one just read, with only MPRV set. This is a transient window: mstatus is modified only for the duration of the unprivileged memory access, then restored exactly. No persistent UXL corruption can occur through this path unless a trap within the critical section corrupts the 'mstatus' variable on the stack, which would be a separate kernel-stack-safety issue outside the scope of this spec.

