PR #2769  overall: potential_risk
Spec: Removing impl-def-behavior from MSTATUS_UXL_LEGAL_VALS_SMODE hardens the legal UXL value set so S-mode cannot operate under an unintended XLEN that would misinterpret U-mode virtual addresses and breach the S/U privilege boundary.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/include/riscvreg.h:82-124  (top-level macro definitions)
      The MSTATUS_* bitfield block defines masks and shifts for UIE, SIE, MIE, FS, XS, MPP, SPP, VM (with legal-value constants like MSTATUS_VM_MBARE/SV39/SV48), and SD, but is entirely missing definitions for MSTATUS_UXL (bits 32-33) and MSTATUS_SXL (bits 34-35). This means the kernel has no symbolic constants to mask, shift, or validate the UXL/SXL fields of sstatus. The kernel currently relies on the unenforced invariant that M-mode firmware (OpenSBI) initializes mstatus.UXL = XLEN_64 on RV64 hardware. If a non-conforming implementation or firmware set an unexpected UXL value, the kernel would silently operate with a mismatched XLEN assumption for user-mode address interpretation. This is directly the vulnerability pattern described in the security spec: without architectural legal-value constants, portable software cannot validate the field.
  [OK] sys/riscv/include/encoding.h:1069-1069  (top-level CSR declarations)
      This line is a single CSR-number declaration: DECLARE_CSR(mstatus, CSR_MSTATUS). It correctly maps the mnemonic 'mstatus' to CSR address 0x300 (defined at encoding.h:577). The declaration itself is accurate and compliant. The absence of accompanying UXL/SXL value-encoding macros (UXL_32, UXL_64, SXL_32, SXL_64) is not a bug at this location — those belong in riscvreg.h alongside the existing VM-mode legal-value constants. No security defect here.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/riscv/identcpu.c:164-193  parse_ext_s
      parse_ext_s parses S-mode ISA extension strings (sstc, svnapot, svpbmt, svinval, sscofpmf) into desc->smode_extensions bitflags. This demonstrates the kernel already has CPU-identification infrastructure with S-mode awareness. However, the function performs no validation of the mstatus.UXL/SXL fields read from the CPU's sstatus CSR. This is a missed integration point: during CPU identification, the kernel could read sstatus, extract UXL (bits 32-33), and verify it matches the compile-time __riscv_xlen. Without this check, a misconfigured implementation goes undetected. The function itself is correct for what it does, but the absence of an adjacent UXL/SXL validation means the kernel's correctness on this critical privilege-boundary field depends solely on the implicit invariant that firmware configured it correctly.
  [OK] sys/riscv/riscv/identcpu.c:611-619  printcpuinfo
      printcpuinfo prints S-mode extension flags from smode_extensions via printf with a %#b bitfield formatter. This is a purely diagnostic function; it has no role in enforcing or validating the security-critical UXL/SXL configuration. The absence of UXL/SXL reporting here is a feature gap (admins cannot observe the current UXL/SXL state), but it is not itself a security vulnerability. The privilege_escape threat described in the spec does not arise from logging omissions.


